SUMMARY:[現場] Praying for Victory: Loyalty\, Dissent\, and Repression in
 side the Russian Orthodox Church Today
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/praying-for-victory-loyalty-dissent-a
 nd-repression-inside-the-russian-orthodox-church-today
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/praying-for-victory-loyalty-d
 issent-and-repression-inside-the-russian-orthodox-church-today\n\nTalk Det
 ails:\nSince 2022 the Moscow Patriarchate has demanded more of its clergy 
 than silence. Every liturgy now includes a prayer for “Holy Rus’” th
 at asks God to grant victory\, and priests who replaced “victory” with
  “peace” have been suspended or defrocked. Believers who speak against
  the war face church courts as well as prosecution for “discrediting” 
 the army\, sometimes after their own parishioners have reported them.\n\nN
 one of this began in 2022. Over three post-Soviet decades the church assem
 bled a language of loyalty piece by piece: the myths of “Holy Rus’” 
 and the “Russian world\,” a memory of the Second World War recast in s
 acred terms\, St. Seraphim of Sarov adopted as patron of the nuclear weapo
 ns complex. Alongside the myths came an imagery that borrows the authority
  of the icon while emptying it of meaning. In 2020 the Main Cathedral of t
 he Russian Armed Forces gathered all of it into one building. Drawing on c
 ases documented between 2022 and 2026 and on fifteen years of work inside 
 the Patriarchate\, Sergei Chapnin traces how this apparatus of images and 
 myths was built\, how it now produces and polices loyalty\, and what disse
 nt looks like when open protest is impossible. What does this crisis revea
 l about the church’s bond with state power\, and what does it leave to O
 rthodox Christians in Russia and abroad?\n\nRSVP here\n\nAbout the Speaker
 :\nSergei Chapnin is a Russian Orthodox journalist\, scholar\, and editor 
 who has written on the Russian Orthodox Church\, church–state
